+# Decision: Change Delivery Approach
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard delivers committed board changes to up to 800 simultaneous browser sessions. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; the server commits each change with an event revision; browsers must display those changes. The delivery mechanism determines how browsers learn about committed changes, which shapes the design of the change store (what indexes and replay capability it needs), the delivery subsystem (what sessions or queries it manages), and browser sync (how browsers subscribe, reconnect, and catch up).
+
+The system must show committed changes within 15 seconds under normal connectivity. Subsecond updates would be useful but are not required. Browsers may be disconnected for up to 20 minutes and must recover the latest board state.
+
+The choice of delivery approach is load-bearing: it affects three of the four major subsystems and determines whether the project takes on connection management operations or incurs recurring vendor costs.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Gate (inherited):** Must support showing committed changes within 15 seconds under normal connectivity. All three candidate approaches meet this threshold.
+
+**Gate (inherited):** Must support 800 simultaneous browsers. All three candidate approaches can scale to this level.
+
+**Weighted criteria:**
+
+- **Update latency** (major) — how quickly committed changes reach connected browsers. Subsecond delivery would be useful. Aligns with the tenet that connectivity failures must be visible: faster updates make stale views easier to detect.
+
+- **Vendor independence** (major) — whether the delivery mechanism couples the project to a third-party realtime service provider. The tenet permits both self-hosted and managed infrastructure, so coupling is not prohibited, but vendor independence preserves deployment flexibility and avoids recurring charges shaped by vendor pricing.
+
+- **Operational complexity** (moderate) — the connection management, reconnect handling, and session state the delivery mechanism requires. Self-operated infrastructure accepts more operational work; stateless mechanisms have less.
+
+- **Recurring service costs** (minor) — whether the approach incurs ongoing charges beyond hosting costs. The project has no declared budget constraint, but avoiding unnecessary recurring fees leaves room for future capability spending.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-Second HTTPS Polling
+
+Browsers poll the server every five seconds for changes since their last-seen revision. The change store provides cursor-based queries returning revisions after a given point. Delivery remains request/response; no sessions are maintained. Browsers run a polling loop in JavaScript.
+
+**Demonstration:**
+```
+GET /events/{event-id}/changes?after=42
+→ {revisions: [43, 44], changes: [...]}
+```
+
+**Analysis:**
+
+- **Update latency:** weak fit. Changes reach browsers in 0–5 seconds depending on poll timing, averaging 2.5 seconds. Meets the 15-second gate with margin but delivers slower than subsecond approaches.
+
+- **Vendor independence:** strong fit. No third-party realtime service. The project owns the entire delivery path.
+
+- **Operational complexity:** strong fit. Stateless request/response. No connection management, no reconnect logic on the server. Browsers handle polling; the server answers queries.
+
+- **Recurring service costs:** strong fit. No vendor charges. Only standard hosting costs.
+
+**Summary:** Simplest stateless operation with 2–5 second delivery lag.
+
+### Self-Hosted WebSockets
+
+The server maintains WebSocket connections to browsers. When a change commits, the server publishes it to connected sessions. The change store maintains replayable revisions; delivery manages WebSocket sessions and handles reconnects. Browsers subscribe on connection and request catch-up from their last-seen revision on reconnect.
+
+**Demonstration:**
+```
+WebSocket /events/{event-id}/live
+→ {type: "change", revision: 43, ...}
+Reconnect: {type: "subscribe", after: 40}
+→ {type: "catchup", revisions: [41, 42, 43], ...}
+```
+
+**Analysis:**
+
+- **Update latency:** strong fit. Committed changes reach browsers in milliseconds, well under one second. Subsecond delivery makes stale views obvious.
+
+- **Vendor independence:** strong fit. No third-party realtime service. The project owns connection handling and can deploy anywhere WebSockets are supported.
+
+- **Operational complexity:** weak fit. Requires connection lifecycle management (connect, disconnect, timeout), session state tracking, publication to active sessions, and reconnect/catch-up protocol. Browsers must implement reconnect logic and revision-based catch-up.
+
+- **Recurring service costs:** strong fit. No vendor charges beyond hosting. WebSocket connections consume server memory and file descriptors, but 800 connections is manageable on standard infrastructure.
+
+**Summary:** Fast updates with self-hosted connection and reconnect operations.
+
+### Hosted Realtime Service
+
+A third-party realtime service (e.g., Pusher, Ably, Firebase) handles browser connections. The server publishes committed changes to the service's API; the service delivers them to subscribed browsers. The change store publishes to the vendor's publication endpoint; delivery wraps the vendor's SDK. Browsers use the vendor's client library for subscription and reconnect.
+
+**Demonstration:**
+```
+Server: POST https://api.vendor.com/publish
+Browser: vendor.subscribe('event-123')
+```
+
+**Analysis:**
+
+- **Update latency:** strong fit. Vendor-managed connections deliver changes in milliseconds, comparable to self-hosted WebSockets.
+
+- **Vendor independence:** eliminated. The delivery subsystem is built around the vendor's SDK and publication API. Changing vendors requires rewriting delivery and browser sync; switching to self-hosted requires building connection management from scratch. The tenet permits managed infrastructure, so this is not a gate failure, but it couples a core capability to one provider's pricing and operational stability.
+
+- **Operational complexity:** partial fit. The vendor handles connection management and reconnect operations, but the project takes on publication reconciliation (ensuring the vendor's API received the change), credential management, and monitoring the vendor's delivery SLA. Lower operational burden than self-hosted sockets, but not zero.
+
+- **Recurring service costs:** weak fit. Charges scale with connected sessions and message volume. At 800 simultaneous browsers with 2 changes per minute average and opening-time bursts, costs would be ongoing and shaped by vendor pricing tiers.
+
+**Summary:** Fast updates with lower connection operations but vendor coupling and recurring fees.
+
+## Selection
+
+Self-hosted WebSockets. This approach provides subsecond update latency without coupling the board to a third-party realtime service or incurring recurring vendor charges. It wins on the two major criteria: update latency (strong fit, delivering changes in milliseconds) and vendor independence (strong fit, owning the entire delivery path). It accepts weaker fit on operational complexity — the project will build connection management, session tracking, and reconnect/catch-up logic — but this work is bounded and contained within two subsystems (delivery and browser sync), and it buys deployment flexibility and cost predictability.
+
+The decision turns on the tenet that both self-hosted and managed infrastructure are permitted, leaning toward self-hosted when vendor coupling has no offsetting advantage. Hosted realtime services offer lower operational complexity but tie a core capability to one provider's pricing and stability; the tradeoff is not favorable here. Five-second HTTPS polling avoids vendor coupling and operational work but delivers changes 5–10x slower; subsecond delivery would be useful, and the operational work to achieve it is acceptable.
+
+**Downstream implications:**
+
+- The change store must maintain replayable revisions indexed by event and revision number so reconnecting browsers can catch up from their last-seen revision.
+- Delivery owns WebSocket session lifecycle (connect, disconnect, timeout), tracks active sessions per event, publishes committed changes to connected sessions, and handles catch-up requests on reconnect.
+- Browser sync implements subscription on connection, revision-based catch-up requests, reconnect with exponential backoff, and visible connectivity state so users can distinguish stale boards from unchanged boards.
